 ## Overview
-Face Culling and Visibility Optimization is a family of techniques used in blocky meshing to reduce the geometric complexity of voxel terrain by eliminating faces that are occluded by adjacent solid blocks or invisible to the camera [S1, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of naively rendering all six faces of every voxel cube, which results in excessive triangle counts and GPU fill-rate waste [S2, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from its siblings is its focus on pre-rasterization optimization: it operates during the mesh construction phase to minimize the vertex and index buffer size sent to the GPU, rather than relying on runtime rasterization culling or post-processing [S1, unofficial].
+Face Culling and Visibility Optimization is a family of mesh generation techniques that reduce the number of rendered primitives in voxel-based rendering by discarding faces that are occluded by adjacent solid voxels or oriented away from the camera [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of naive rendering, where every voxel generates six faces (12 triangles), including those hidden by neighbors, by skipping the addition of internal faces to the vertex buffer [S2, unofficial]. Unlike view-dependent methods, basic face culling is a static, geometry-based optimization performed during mesh creation, resulting in no framerate loss during rendering [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family share the core principle of reducing the number of rendered primitives by identifying and discarding geometry that does not contribute to the final image. The fundamental mechanism involves checking the occupancy or visibility state of neighboring voxels or camera viewpoints to determine if a face should be generated.
+The core mechanism of this family is the determination of face visibility based on local voxel topology or global camera constraints.
 
-### Neighbor-Based Culling
-The most common approach is neighbor-based culling. For a voxel at position $P$ with a face normal $N$, the method checks the voxel at $P_{neighbor} = P + N$. If $P_{neighbor}$ is occupied by a solid block, the face at $P$ in direction $N$ is internal and is culled [S1, unofficial]. If $P_{neighbor}$ is empty (air) or out of bounds, the face is potentially visible and is added to the mesh [S2, unofficial].
+### Topological Culling (Neighbor-Based)
+The most common form of face culling relies on the occupancy state of neighboring voxels. For a voxel at position $P$ with a face normal $N$, the neighbor position is calculated as:
+$$ P_{neighbor} = P + N $$
+If $P_{neighbor}$ is occupied by a solid block, the face at $P$ in direction $N$ is internal and is culled (not added to the mesh) [S1, S2, unofficial]. If $P_{neighbor}$ is empty (air) or out of bounds, the face is potentially visible and is added to the vertex buffer [S2, unofficial]. This logic applies to all six faces of a voxel.
 
-### View-Dependent Culling
-Some methods extend neighbor checks with view-dependent tests. This involves determining the orientation of a face relative to the camera view direction. If a face is back-facing (the dot product of the face normal and the view direction is positive, indicating it faces away from the camera), it is culled [S1, unofficial]. This is often combined with internal face masking to reject shared faces between neighboring voxels [S1, unofficial].
+### View-Dependent Culling (Backface)
+Some methods extend topological culling with view-dependent backface culling. This discards faces oriented away from the camera. In a shader-based approach, the visibility is determined by transforming voxel corners to clip space and checking the dot product of the face normal (or an approximation thereof) with the view direction [S1, unofficial]. If the dot product indicates the face is facing away, it is discarded [S1, unofficial].
 
-### Multi-View Consistency
-For non-voxelized or complex meshes, visibility is determined by projecting vertices into multiple camera views and comparing their depths against reference depth maps or binary masks. A vertex or face is retained only if it is consistently visible across a threshold number of views [S1, unofficial].
+### Data Structures for State
+Efficient implementation often requires compact data structures to track visibility:
+*   **Bitmasks:** A 32-bit integer per voxel or chunk can store visibility masks for the six faces (bits 0-5) and position data (bits 6-30) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Buried Bitmasks:** A bitmask can track which blocks in a chunk are "buried" (fully surrounded by opaque blocks), allowing the renderer to skip mesh generation for those entire blocks [S2, unofficial].
 
